{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1788368651,"nonce":"N8HRle3ARfo5EkbzwDPGdJFSOSg=","target":"a8d8ada9794e947f6d0ebb6babbfa7d1fe767778388df5b0fc5db6cf2047cce2","message":"Observed live on the bench, 2026-09-02, while proving `eef837a`. A fresh `Corpse_Cat` was unforbidden, the butcher bill went `workable`, the run advanced ~80,000 ticks — and the corpse was **gone**. No `Meat_Cat`, no butcher job, bill back to `asleep-no-matching-ingredient` with `scanned: 2`.\n\nA wild animal ate it. Ordinary RimWorld. But from the protocol's side the thing ceased to exist between two calls with nothing anywhere saying so.\n\nEvan's framing sets the scope: *\"all that matters from this is that the corpse got disposed of and can't be eaten anymore, so simplicity over fixing every case, the agent just has to not panic and think the game broke.\"*\n\n\nThe first instinct was a missing-part fraction (`Verse/HediffSet.GetMissingPartsCommonAncestors`), which is real and readable — a predator eats a corpse progressively.\n\n**It does not solve this.** That fraction dies with the thing. Once the corpse is destroyed, `things --def Corpse_Cat` returns `count: 0` and there is no row left to carry a percentage. So it only helps an agent that happened to look *while* the cat was being eaten, and helps not at all at the moment it vanishes — which is the moment the agent needs it.\n\nEvan, on being shown this: *\"they call for the cat again and it says something about how the % went to 0 and they disappeared?\"* There is nothing to call. That is the whole problem.\n\n\n- **The journal is history and survives the thing.** A destroyed corpse cannot carry a field; an append-only event about it can.\n- **The agent is FORCED to read the journal every turn** by `722c951`'s `unread-journal` guard. A journal event is guaranteed to be seen. A field on a row that no longer exists is guaranteed not to be.\n- `JournalHooks.cs` already Harmony-patches ten members and the journal already carries twelve event types. This is one more of an established kind, not a new mechanism.\n- **`Thing.Destroy(DestroyMode mode)` hands us the category for free**: `Vanish`, `WillReplace`, `KillFinalize`, `KillFinalizeLeavingsOnly`, `Deconstruct`, `FailConstruction`, `Cancel`, `Refund`, `QuestLogic`. No forensics, no guessing at predators, no distinguishing fire from teeth — the game already says which kind of ending it was.\n\n\n1. A journal event when a thing **of interest** is destroyed, carrying `def`, `at`, `id` and the `DestroyMode`. Cite the patched member.\n2. **\"Of interest\" must be narrow and stated.** `Thing.Destroy` is extremely hot — every item, every stack merge. Do not patch it globally. Corpses first, since that is the observed case; extend only with a stated reason. If the hot path cannot be made cheap enough, **park it and say so** rather than shipping a per-item hook on a 38-mod bench.\n3. The `DestroyMode` is reported as the game's own value, not re-interpreted into a story. `Vanish` is not \"eaten by a predator\" — it is `Vanish`, and the agent can branch on it.\n4. `checklists/turn.md`'s journal read gains one line: a `destroyed` event for something you were planning around means it is gone; re-plan rather than re-query.\n5. Bench check as a markdown command list, not a Python suite: leave a fresh corpse where wildlife will take it, advance, assert the journal carries the event with a mode.\n\n\nThe missing-part fraction on a **live** corpse row, as a leading indicator — \"this corpse is 60% gone, stop planning a bill around it.\" Nice, cheap, and strictly secondary to item 1. Ship item 1 alone if there is any doubt.\n\n\n`eef837a` killed three colonists because the agent could not see why a bill would not run. This is the same class one step out: the agent now gets a correct `no-matching-ingredient` verdict but cannot tell \"your ingredient was eaten\" from \"the game is behaving strangely.\" The cost of the wrong conclusion is a run that stops trusting its own instruments.\n\nRelated: `f1a1700` (parked) is the room-and-building half of \"things vanish silently\" and is blocked on room identity. This is the thing half and is much cheaper — a `Destroy` hook needs no durable handle, only the moment.","files":null}]}